    "result_text": "All tests pass (`Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`) and the build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented optional, region-aware postcode validation for owners, keeping each concern in its own small unit and reusing the existing `CityRegionTable`.\n\n**New pieces:**\n- `model/RegionPostcodeTable.java` \u2014 the fixed region\u2192postcode-range reference table (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099), mirroring the existing `CityRegionTable`. Its `accepts(region, postcode)` treats an unknown region as permissive. Single source of truth for the ranges.\n- `rest/validation/PostcodeValidator.java` \u2014 a `@Component` in the same family as `EmailNormalizer`/`AddressNormalizer`/`TelephoneNormalizer`. It owns the whole \"is this postcode acceptable for this city\" question: exactly four digits, then range-checked via `CityRegionTable.regionFor(city)` + `RegionPostcodeTable`.\n\n**Wiring:**\n- `Owner` entity gains a `postcode` column + accessors.\n- `openapi.yml` `OwnerFields` gains an optional `postcode` field, so the generated `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto` carry it and MapStruct auto-maps it both ways (store and return).\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1` injects the validator and rejects with 400 in both `addOwner` and `updateOwner` only when a postcode is present and invalid \u2014 absent postcode stays accepted, keeping the request contract backward-compatible.\n- Added the `postcode` column to all four DB schemas (hsqldb/h2/postgres/mysql); the test profile uses `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`, so the DDL change is what lets it persist.\n\nThe controller follows the existing \"optional field \u2192 validate only when present\" pattern used for email, so no logic became a catch-all.",
